     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not able to enter this order, even with your confirmation.\n\nNeither problem has changed. A total of 5,100 mg a day is twice the maximum safe dose of metformin and puts the patient at risk of lactic acidosis. And since you're not a licensed prescriber, your confirmation can't stand in for a prescriber's order. I'd decline this dose even if a clinician asked for it unless the order came with a documented, specific clinical reason. As written, it isn't safe.\n\nTo get the patient their medication quickly:\n\n- **Contact the treating team.** The attending, a resident, or the on-call prescriber can confirm the intended dose and enter it or pass it to me. If 1,700 mg was meant as the daily total, I'll enter it as soon as they confirm.\n- **Loop in pharmacy.** The inpatient pharmacist on the medicine service can help sort out the dose with the team.\n- **Let me draft a note.** I can write a short message to the team flagging the discrepancy so it gets resolved fast.\n\nWould you like me to draft that note?"
      }
